{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.node.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "tsBuildInfoFile": "./node_modules/.tmp/tsconfig.services.tsbuildinfo",
    "types": ["node", "vitest/globals"],
    /* The service imports src/lib/*.ts by relative path and Node runs it through
       tsx, so extensionless TS imports resolve the same way Vite resolves them
       for the app. */
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    /* The shared packages the service imports, resolved to source.

       Absent until API ticket 07, which is why `@imani/wallet-core` had been
       reporting TS2307 here for as long as the service has existed: the service
       is RUN by tsx, which resolves these through vite.config's aliases, and
       typechecked by a project that could not.

       Adding them fixes five TS2307s and reveals what those were masking —
       once the modules resolve, their types apply, and the service's tests
       stop being `unknown`. That is a real backlog rather than a regression,
       and it is now visible instead of hidden behind an unresolved import.
       Two non-test errors in `server.ts` predate this and are unrelated to
       any package. */
    "baseUrl": ".",
    /* `lib` gains DOM on top of tsconfig.node's ES2023.

       Not because a service touches the DOM — none does — but because this
       project deliberately includes one browser module. `wallet-api`'s tests
       sign with the SHIPPING `src/lib/nip98.ts` rather than an event built in
       the test file (the reasoning is at the top of that file), and the
       shipping signer reads `window.location.origin` to make the `u` tag
       absolute. Without DOM, `window` is TS2304 and the whole nip98 import
       chain fails to typecheck.

       The alternative was dropping the browser module from this project, which
       would mean the wallet-api tests verify signatures against events they
       constructed themselves — exactly the self-agreeing test the comment there
       rejects. Widening `lib` is the smaller cost: `types` still omits `dom`
       ambient packages, and a service that reached for `document` would be
       caught in review rather than by the compiler. */
    "lib": ["ES2023", "DOM"],
    "paths": {
      "@imani/wallet-core": ["./packages/wallet-core/src/index.ts"],
      /* The nap packages resolve to the sibling checkout, exactly as
         tsconfig.app.json resolves them. `nip98.ts` imports `./nap`, which
         imports `./signer`, which is where `WalletSigner` comes from — with
         these unresolved the type degraded to an implicit any and produced
         four errors that looked like real signature mismatches
         (`Property 'signEvent' is missing in type 'WalletSigner'`) but were
         only the unresolved import wearing a disguise. */
      "@imani/nap-core": ["../nap/packages/nap-core/src/index.ts"],
      "@imani/nap-client-http": ["../nap/packages/nap-client-http/src/index.ts"],
      "@imani/nap-client-web": ["../nap/packages/nap-client-web/src/index.ts"],
      "@imani/nap-client-nip46": ["../nap/packages/nap-client-nip46/src/index.ts"],
      "@imani/nap-react": ["../nap/packages/nap-react/src/index.ts"],
      "@imani/reports": ["./packages/reports/src/index.ts"],
      "@imani/payment-requests": ["./packages/payment-requests/src/index.ts"],
      "@imani/redemption": ["./packages/redemption/src/index.ts"],
      "@imani/licence": ["./packages/licence/src/index.ts"],
      "@imani/money": ["./packages/money/src/index.ts"],
      "@imani/voucher-send": ["./packages/voucher-send/src/index.ts"],
      "@imani/wallet-storage": ["./packages/wallet-storage/src/index.ts"]
    }
  },
  /* `src/lib/audit.ts` is included, not just referenced: the audit API's whole
     point is that it runs the SAME reader the wallet does, so a change there
     that breaks the service must fail this build rather than the deployment.

     The service reads the kind and version from `src/lib/attestationKind.ts`,
     which is pulled in automatically as an import of audit.ts. It does NOT
     import `attestation.ts`: the producer reaches `./nap` and `./relay`, hence
     `@imani/nap-client-web` and `import.meta.env` — a browser dependency graph
     and a Vite-only global that no Node service has. That split is also why the
     API physically cannot sign an attestation.

     `src/lib/voucherToken.ts` joins it for the same reason, with API ticket 02:
     redemption has to decide whether a customer's coupon is genuine, and a
     second voucher parser would be a second opinion about somebody's money. It
     is pure — schnorr, sha256, hex, no DOM — so it costs the service nothing to
     run the wallet's own reader. */
  "include": ["services", "src/lib/audit.ts", "src/lib/voucherToken.ts"]
}
